# The art dump: every candidate three image models produced, kept locally as the
# provenance record for what is in assets/source/. 211 MB, and nothing builds from
# it -- tools/build-terrain.js reads assets/source/ only. See docs/art-brief.md.
assets/source/dump/

# Raw plates as the image models returned them: 1254-2048px and 2.6-8.4 MB each. The tracked art in
# assets/source/ runs to 3 MB a file, so one of these would be in keeping -- but the queue in
# docs/plate-prompts.md is fifty-six long, and 145 MB of inputs nothing ships is too much weight for
# a browser game. tools/build-plates.js writes the ~100 KB version that does ship, and that is
# committed. Keep the raws; re-running the build at a different size needs them.
assets/source/plates/

# Raw portraits, on exactly the same footing and for the same reason: 784-2048px and 1.8-8.7 MB
# each, against the ~50 KB the build writes into src/ui/portraits/ and ships. Eight people rather
# than fifty-six species, so the total is smaller -- but a raw is still an input nothing ships, and
# the rule that keeps plates out should not have an exception for people. Keep them on disk;
# rebuilding at another size needs them.
assets/source/portraits/

# Local performance baseline: describes a machine, not the code.
.perf-baseline.json

# Reference media: pictures we look at, that nothing in the repo reads.
#
# `endgame.png` is the target frame the whole rendering programme was measured against. It is named
# in three docs and three source comments and opened by nothing -- it is a thing a person looks at,
# and 2.5 MB of a person looking at something does not need to be in everybody's clone.
#
# Kept on disk rather than deleted, the same arrangement as assets/source/dump/: that directory is
# the provenance record for the art, and this is the provenance record for the brief.
dump/
